Retry the write wait's poll after EINTR

uWSGI 2.0.31 waits for a socket in two hooks. The read wait,
`uwsgi_simple_wait_read_hook` in core/reader.c, calls `poll()` again
when a signal interrupts it. The write wait,
`uwsgi_simple_wait_write_hook` in core/writer.c, returns the error: it
logs `uwsgi_simple_wait_write_hook()/poll(): Interrupted system call`,
the write fails with `OSError: write error`, and the worker drops the
response.

The CobaltCore API containers run uWSGI with
`--hook-master-start "unix_signal:15 gracefully_kill_them_all"`
(`UWSGIGracefulShutdownArgs` in internal/common/deployment/uwsgi.go).
On SIGTERM the master sends SIGHUP to every worker, and each worker
finishes its current request before it exits. That SIGHUP interrupts a
worker that waits for a slow client to take a streamed response, so
without this patch the graceful shutdown cuts the stream. Uploads are
not affected, because the read wait retries.

Measured with uWSGI 2.0.31 built from the sdist with and without this
hunk (python:3.12-bookworm, the three flags of
`UWSGIGracefulShutdownArgs`, a 128 MiB response with Content-Length, a
client reading at 64 MiB/s that sends SIGTERM 0.5 s after the first
body byte): unpatched delivered about 42 MB and logged the EINTR line
in 3 of 3 runs; patched delivered all 134,217,728 bytes, exited 0 and
logged `goodbye to uWSGI.` in 3 of 3. 24 MB at 4 MB/s gave 21.3 MB
unpatched and every byte patched; 100 MB at 50 MB/s gave 65 MB
unpatched and every byte patched.

The patch retries `poll()` after EINTR the way the read wait does. The
timeout restarts with each retry, as it does in the read wait. The
master still SIGKILLs the HTTP router once the last worker exits, so a
client too slow for the router to flush its buffer within about a
second still loses the tail of the response.

`assert_uwsgi_graceful_shutdown` in tests/lib/uwsgi.sh, which the
verify scripts of the eight uWSGI API images call, streams 128 MiB
through SIGTERM and fails on a short body or on the EINTR line.

nix/lib/python-env.nix applies the patch to the uwsgi source of every
lock with `patch --fuzz=0`. A uWSGI release that moves these lines, or
already carries the fix, fails the build there.

Applies to uWSGI 2.0.31 (nix/base-requirements.txt).

Upstream status: not reported; tracked in #1635.

